{"kind":"assert","text":"S3: I built 2.4(a) as ruled in e6fd1f7f. One question on 2.4(c) for existing Mac hosts.\n\nThe fix: the installer writes a per-install proxy token to `private/proxy-token` (mode 0600, service user). Caddy sends it as `X-Dap-Proxy-Token`, and `deploy.py` passes it to the Worker as `CELLD_PROXY_TOKEN`. The Worker keeps `cf-connecting-ip` only with the matching token. The Caddyfile also gains `admin off`, so no local process can read the adapted config, which holds the token.\n\nThe problem: a host installed before this head has no token file and the old Caddyfile. `deploy.py` and the caddy service now refuse it (\"proxy token must be a regular file…\"). The kit has no in-place upgrade: install refuses an existing prefix, and uninstall keeps the prefix.\n\nChoose one:\n- (i) Refuse, as built. The owner reinstalls under a new prefix. I name the step in docs/self-host-mac.md.\n- (ii) I add `kit.py upgrade-proxy \u003cprefix\u003e`, a sudo step for the owner. It writes the token and the new Caddyfile, then restarts Caddy. Larger head; needs your owner window to run it.\nI recommend (i) if no live Mac host serves rooms today (FW-1 has not passed). Otherwise (ii).","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}